@krystianhub have you enabled `smartmon` on the agent side?
It is opt-in, since it can have a negative impact on HDD idle time.

[services.beszel.agent.smartmon.enable](https://search.nixos.org/options?channel=unstable&include_modular_service_options=1&include_nixos_options=1&query=services.beszel.agent&show=option:services.beszel.agent.smartmon.enable)

issue.body:
### Nixpkgs version

- Stable (25.11)

### Describe the bug

The **beszel** package in nixpkgs is currently built with the **testing** build tag:

https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/blob/10e7ad5bbcb421fe07e3a4ad53a634b0cd57ffac/pkgs/by-name/be/beszel/package.nix#L56

This causes Beszel Hub to not collect any SMART monitoring data. In the UI, the "SMART" tab remains empty and no disks are shown.

With the **testing** tag enabled, the following code is compiled:

https://github.com/henrygd/beszel/blob/c1c1cd1bcbeb234c5735bb02d527ff76b60e7c49/internal/hub/systems/systems_test_helpers.go#L18
```
func backgroundSmartFetchEnabled() bool { return false }
```

This effectively disables SMART data collection entirely.

When the **testing** tag is not present, a different implementation is used:

https://github.com/henrygd/beszel/blob/c1c1cd1bcbeb234c5735bb02d527ff76b60e7c49/internal/hub/systems/systems_production.go#L9
```
func backgroundSmartFetchEnabled() bool { return true }
```

In this case, the hub correctly collects SMART data from connected agents, and the "SMART" tab in the UI is populated.

Overriding the package to remove the **testing** tag fixes the issue:

```
  services.beszel.hub = {
    enable = true;
    package = pkgs.beszel.overrideAttrs (prev: {
      doCheck = false;
      tags = [ ];
    });
  };
```

### Steps to reproduce

- Enable Beszel Hub:
```
  services.beszel.hub = {
    enable = true;
    port = 8905;
  };
```

- Go to localhost:8905 and connect new agent; note SSH key and token

- Enable Beszel Agent:
```
 services.beszel.agent = {
    enable = true;
    environment = {
      HUB_URL = "http://127.0.0.1:8905";
      TOKEN = "[... fill in from the Beszel Hub ...]";
      KEY = "[... fill in from the Beszel Hub ...]";
    };
```

- Go back to Beszel Hub, click on SMART data tab (http://localhost:8905/smart)
- Wait a few minutes, observe no disks listed

### Expected behaviour

SMART data should show up automatically in the Beszel Hub from connected agents.
After a few minutes, it should appear in the UI at: http://localhost:8905/smart

### Screenshots

_No response_

### Relevant log output

```console

```

### Additional context

I initially suspected systemd permissions around smartctl, but ruled that out.

To investigate further, I instrumented the beszel binary with additional **fmt.Println** debugging to understand why no SMART data was being collected - even with **LOG_LEVEL=debug**, there were no relevant errors.

This led me to the following code path:
https://github.com/henrygd/beszel/blob/c1c1cd1bcbeb234c5735bb02d527ff76b60e7c49/internal/hub/systems/system.go#L157
```
// Fetch and save SMART devices when system first comes online or at intervals
	if backgroundSmartFetchEnabled() && sys.detailsFetched.Load() {
```

The condition evaluates to false because **backgroundSmartFetchEnabled()** returns false when the binary is built with the **testing** tag.

As a result, the SMART collector is never executed, which explains the complete absence of SMART data in the UI.

